[0.115378, "o", "AI Provider: \u001b[33mcustomrest\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m0\u001b[0m: \u001b[93mNode\u001b[0m \u001b[33mworker-3\u001b[0m(\u001b[36m\u001b[0m)\r\n- \u001b[31mError:\u001b[0m \u001b[31mworker-3 has condition of type DiskPressure, reason KubeletHasNoDiskSpace: kubelet has disk pressure\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32mOf course. This is a very common and critical issue in Kubernetes clusters. Let's break down what this error means, why it's a big problem, and how to systematically fix it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 1. What Does the Error Mean?\r\n\r\n*   **`worker-3`**: This is the name of one of your worker nodes in the cluster.\r\n*   **`DiskPressure`**: This is a **node condition**. Kubernetes sets this condition on a node when the kubelet (the agent running on the node) detects that the node's disk is under severe stress.\r\n*   **`KubeletHasNoDiskSpace`**: This is the specific reason for the `DiskPressure` condition. It means the kubelet itself is running out of disk space.\r\n*   **`kubelet has disk pressure`**: This is a human-readable summary. The kubelet has determined that the available disk space is critically low.\r\n\r\nIn simple terms: **The disk on your `worker-3` node is almost full, and the Kubernetes system has marked it as unhealthy and unable to accept new work.**\r\n\r\n### 2. Why is This a Critical Problem?\r\n\r\nWhen a node is in `DiskPressure`, the Kubernetes control plane will take several actions to protect the cluster:\r\n\r\n1.  **Pod Scheduling**: The scheduler will **not** schedule any new pods onto this node. It's effectively quarantined from new work.\r\n2.  **Eviction**: The kubelet will start **evicting** existing pods from the node to free up space. It prioritizes pods based on their Quality of Service (QoS) tier:\r\n    *   **BestEffort** (evicted first)\r\n    *   **Burstable**\r\n    *   **Guaranteed** (evicted last, as they are considered the most critical)\r\n3.  **Instability**: The node itself may become unstable. The kubelet, container runtime (Docker/Containerd), and other system processes can crash or malfunction when they run out of disk space, leading to a complete node failure.\r\n\r\n### 3. How to Diagnose and Fix the Issue\r\n\r\nFollow these steps in order. The goal is to first understand what is using the disk, then clean it up, and finally prevent it from happening again.\r\n\r\n#### Step 1: SSH into the Problematic Node\r\n\r\nConnect to the `worker-3` node via SSH.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nssh user@worker-3\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### Step 2: Check Disk Usage\r\n\r\nIdentify which partition is full. The most critical partition for this issue is usually `/var/lib/kubelet` or `/var/lib/docker`.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# See overall disk usage\r\ndf -h\r\n\r\n# A more detailed view, including inodes (which can also fill up)\r\ndf -i\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou will likely see that the `/` or `/var` partition is at or near 100% capacity.\r\n\r\n#### Step 3: Find the Culprit (What's Filling the Disk?)\r\n\r\nThe space is almost certainly being consumed by one of these:\r\n*   **Container Images**: Old, unused images.\r\n*   **Container Log Files**: Uncontrolled logging that fills up the disk.\r\n*   **EmptyDir Volumes**: Pods that have crashed but their `emptyDir` volumes are still mounted and holding data.\r\n*   **Kubelet State**: Old pod sandboxes or other state that hasn't been cleaned up.\r\n\r\nLet's investigate each one.\r\n\r\n**A. Check Container Logs (Most Common Culprit)**\r\n\r\nLogs are the #1 cause of disk pressure. Use `crictl` (the standard CRI tool) to find logs for containers that are using a lot of space.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# List all containers and their log sizes\r\ncrictl ps --no-headers -q | xargs -n 1 crictl inspect --output json | jq '.status.logPath' -r | xargs du -sh\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis command will show you the size of each container's log file. Look for any that are several Gigabytes (GB) in size.\r\n\r\n**To fix this:**\r\n*   **Short-term**: Manually truncate the largest log files. **Be careful not to delete a file a process is writing to.** Truncating is safer.\r\n    ```bash\r\n    # Find the largest log file\r\n    find /var/log/containers -name \"*.log\" -exec du -sh {} \\; | sort -rh | head -n 5\r\n\r\n    # Truncate the file (set its size to 0)\r\n"]
[0.118696, "o", "    sudo truncate -s 0 /var/log/containers/huge-log-file.log\r\n    ```\r\n*   **Long-term**: Configure log rotation for your containers. This is done via the Kubelet configuration. See the \"Prevention\" section below.\r\n\r\n**B. Check Kubelet and Docker/Containerd Directories**\r\n\r\nThese directories can fill up with old data.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# Check the size of key directories\r\ndu -sh /var/lib/kubelet/\r\ndu -sh /var/lib/docker/  # If you use Docker\r\ndu -sh /var/lib/containerd/ # If you use containerd\r\n```\r\n\r\n**To fix this:**\r\n*   **Clean up old images**: The kubelet has a built-in mechanism for this.\r\n    ```bash\r\n    # Force a garbage collection of unused images\r\n    sudo crictl rmi --prune\r\n    ```\r\n*   **Clean up unused containers**: The kubelet should do this automatically, but you can force it.\r\n    ```bash\r\n    # Force a cleanup of 'dead' containers\r\n    sudo crictl rmi --prune\r\n    ```\r\n\r\n**C. Check for Stuck `emptyDir` Volumes**\r\n\r\nIf a pod using an `emptyDir` volume crashes, the volume can persist and hold data until the pod is fully deleted.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# List all pods and their volumes\r\nkubectl get pods --all-namespaces -o wide\r\n\r\n# Find pods in a CrashLoop or other non-running state\r\nkubectl get pods --all-namespaces | grep -E CrashLoopBackOff|ImagePullBackOff|ErrImagePull\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf you find a problematic pod, delete it to reclaim the space from its `emptyDir`.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nkubectl delete pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### Step 4: Reclaim Space and Verify\r\n\r\nAfter cleaning up the likely culprits, check the disk usage again.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ndf -h\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou should see a significant amount of free space. The `DiskPressure` condition on the node will not clear immediately. The Kubernetes control plane needs to re-evaluate the node's status, which can take a few minutes. You can check the status from the control plane:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nkubectl describe node worker-3\r\n```\r\n\r\nLook for the `Conditions` section. The `DiskPressure` status should change from `True` to `False`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### 4. How to Prevent DiskPressure in the Future\r\n\r\nCleaning up is a temporary fix. You need to configure your cluster to manage disk space proactively.\r\n\r\n#### 1. Configure Log Rotation (Most Important)\r\n\r\nThis is the most effective preventative measure. You need to tell the Kubelet how to manage logs.\r\n\r\nEdit the Kubelet configuration file (location varies by OS; common paths are `/etc/kubernetes/kubelet.conf`, `/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml`, or passed via `--config` flag).\r\n\r\nAdd or modify these settings:\r\n\r\n```yaml\r\n# Example Kubelet configuration\r\napiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1\r\nkind: KubeletConfiguration\r\n# ... other settings\r\nserverTLSBootstrap: true\r\nevictionHard:\r\n  # ... other eviction thresholds\r\n  nodefs.available: \"10%\"  # Evict if 10% of disk is free\r\n  nodefs.inodesFree: \"5%\"   # Evict if 5% of inodes are free\r\nimageGCHighThresholdPercent: 70  # Run GC when disk usage is > 70%\r\nimageGCLowThresholdPercent: 50   # Stop GC when disk usage is < 50%\r\nmaximumDeadContainersPerPod: 3    # Keep a few dead containers for debugging\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe key settings for logs are often controlled by the container runtime (Docker/Containerd). For Docker, you would configure its `log-opts`. For a more robust solution, use a dedicated logging sidecar like **Fluentd** or **Promtail**, which can be configured to ship logs to a central store (like Loki or Elasticsearch) and manage local log rotation.\r\n\r\n#### 2. Set Eviction Thresholds\r\n\r\nThe `evictionHard` setting in the Kubelet config tells the kubelet *when* to start evicting pods to prevent a crash. The default thresholds might be too high. The example above is a good starting point.\r\n\r\n#### 3. Implement Liveness and Readiness Probes\r\n\r\nProbes help Kubernetes detect and restart unhealthy applications before they crash and leave behind large log files or stuck `emptyDir` volumes.\r\n\r\n#### 4. Use Resource Requests and Limits\r\n\r\nBy setting resource requests and limits for your applications, you ensure they run on nodes with sufficient resources and prevent a single \"noisy neighbor\" from consuming all "]
[0.118775, "o", "available disk space.\r\n\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.118965, "o", "\r\n"]
